1
00:00:00,520 --> 00:00:02,960
Take a look around your house.

2
00:00:03,230 --> 00:00:07,550
Maybe there's a wrench in the garage that says Craftsman.

3
00:00:07,570 --> 00:00:10,690
A washing machine that says Kenmore.

4
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:14,510
And maybe, a few streets away, there's a house

5
00:00:14,530 --> 00:00:18,070
that arrived by train, in thousands of pieces,

6
00:00:18,090 --> 00:00:22,120
with a book of instructions to put it together.

7
00:00:22,390 --> 00:00:25,400
All of it came from the same place.

8
00:00:25,420 --> 00:00:29,540
A company in Chicago called Sears, Roebuck.

9
00:00:29,810 --> 00:00:34,670
In 1886, a railroad station agent in Minnesota

10
00:00:34,690 --> 00:00:40,070
started selling watches by mail, at $14 apiece.

11
00:00:40,340 --> 00:00:46,210
Within a few decades, his company would sell you almost anything.

12
00:00:46,230 --> 00:00:51,420
A dress. A plow. A doll for Christmas. A whole house.

13
00:00:51,690 --> 00:00:56,040
For millions of families on farms and in small towns,

14
00:00:56,060 --> 00:00:59,080
the Sears catalog was the store.

15
00:00:59,350 --> 00:01:03,570
People like to call Sears the Amazon of its time.

16
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:09,790
Then, in 1993, Sears stopped printing its big catalog.

17
00:01:09,810 --> 00:01:13,940
The next year, Amazon was founded.

18
00:01:14,210 --> 00:01:17,610
This is the story of what happened to Sears.

19
00:01:17,630 --> 00:01:22,510
Every photograph you'll see is real, every number has a source,

20
00:01:22,530 --> 00:01:26,010
and you'll see the actual catalog pages.

21
00:01:26,280 --> 00:01:30,450
We'll tell it through seven things Sears sold.

22
00:01:30,470 --> 00:01:34,000
A watch. The catalog. A house. A store.

23
00:01:34,020 --> 00:01:39,040
A tower. The last catalog. And a closing-sale sign.

24
00:01:39,310 --> 00:01:43,410
If you ever waited for the Christmas catalog to arrive,

25
00:01:43,430 --> 00:01:47,770
and circled the toys you wanted, this one is for you.

26
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:56,380
North Redwood, Minnesota. 1886.

27
00:01:56,400 --> 00:01:59,910
Richard Warren Sears is 22 years old,

28
00:01:59,930 --> 00:02:04,400
and he's the agent at the railroad station.

29
00:02:04,670 --> 00:02:09,260
One day, a shipment of watches arrives for a jeweler

30
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:13,290
in a nearby town. The jeweler refuses it.

31
00:02:13,560 --> 00:02:18,560
Instead of sending the watches back, Sears buys them himself.

32
00:02:18,580 --> 00:02:23,500
He offers them to other station agents up and down the line,

33
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:26,780
at $14 each, and they sell them on.

34
00:02:27,050 --> 00:02:31,020
Within six months, he has made $5,000.

35
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:37,110
So he leaves the railroad, and starts a watch company.

36
00:02:37,380 --> 00:02:43,030
In 1887, he moves it to Chicago, where the railroads meet,

37
00:02:43,050 --> 00:02:47,630
and hires a young watch repairman from Indiana.

38
00:02:47,650 --> 00:02:51,260
His name is Alvah Curtis Roebuck.

39
00:02:51,530 --> 00:02:56,160
This is one of their advertisements, from a newspaper

40
00:02:56,180 --> 00:02:59,670
in Winchester, Indiana, in January 1888.

41
00:02:59,690 --> 00:03:02,660
Look at the promise at the bottom.

42
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:07,190
You could see the watch before you paid for it.

43
00:03:07,460 --> 00:03:12,480
In 1893, the company takes both their names.

44
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:15,980
Sears, Roebuck and Company.

45
00:03:16,250 --> 00:03:20,550
Sears writes the advertising, almost all of it himself.

46
00:03:20,570 --> 00:03:24,560
And he finds that people far from any city will buy

47
00:03:24,580 --> 00:03:29,530
almost anything by mail, as long as they trust the sender.

48
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:34,810
In 1895, Roebuck sells out. A Chicago clothing man

49
00:03:34,830 --> 00:03:40,160
named Julius Rosenwald buys in, and turns a brilliant

50
00:03:40,180 --> 00:03:44,090
salesman's business into a machine.

51
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:47,810
Here are the men in charge that year.

52
00:03:47,830 --> 00:03:54,210
Sears, the president. Aaron Nusbaum, the manager. And Rosenwald.

53
00:03:54,480 --> 00:04:02,070
Between 1895 and 1907, sales grow from $750,000 a year

54
00:04:02,090 --> 00:04:05,990
to more than $50 million.

55
00:04:06,260 --> 00:04:11,390
So if a watch could travel by mail to a farm in Minnesota,

56
00:04:11,410 --> 00:04:13,210
what else could?

57
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:23,320
This is the Sears catalog for the spring of 1897.

58
00:04:23,340 --> 00:04:27,540
The company called it the Consumers Guide.

59
00:04:27,810 --> 00:04:35,880
Not long before, it had been a little book of watches and jewelry.

60
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:39,440
By 1894, it ran to 322 pages.

61
00:04:39,460 --> 00:04:42,040
A year later, 532.

62
00:04:42,310 --> 00:04:47,060
Groceries. Stoves. Dolls. Medicines.

63
00:04:47,080 --> 00:04:50,900
Bicycles. Clothing. Plows.

64
00:04:51,170 --> 00:04:54,850
And it reached people where the stores couldn't.

65
00:04:54,870 --> 00:04:58,790
In 1896, the Post Office began Rural Free Delivery.

66
00:04:58,810 --> 00:05:03,570
For the first time, a farm family's mail came to its own gate,

67
00:05:03,590 --> 00:05:06,750
instead of waiting for them in town.

68
00:05:07,020 --> 00:05:09,330
In 1913 came Parcel Post,

69
00:05:09,350 --> 00:05:14,400
and the mail carrier could bring the packages too.

70
00:05:14,670 --> 00:05:21,370
By 1908, more than one in five Americans received the catalog.

71
00:05:21,390 --> 00:05:27,620
At its peak, it offered 100,000 items, on 1,400 pages.

72
00:05:27,890 --> 00:05:33,050
To fill the orders, Sears built a plant on Chicago's West Side.

73
00:05:33,070 --> 00:05:36,920
When it opened in 1906, its mail-order building

74
00:05:36,940 --> 00:05:41,350
was the largest commercial building in the world.

75
00:05:41,620 --> 00:05:44,420
Railroad tracks ran right into it.

76
00:05:44,440 --> 00:05:48,730
Orders rode on belts and chutes from floor to floor,

77
00:05:48,750 --> 00:05:51,790
and parcels left by the carload.

78
00:05:52,060 --> 00:05:56,370
And the catalog printed its customers' letters.

79
00:05:56,390 --> 00:06:02,210
Here's one, from Chapel Hill, North Carolina, January 1897.

80
00:06:02,480 --> 00:06:07,490
“I am much pleased. So far it is all you claim for it.

81
00:06:07,510 --> 00:06:10,130
It keeps splendid time.”

82
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:14,000
At the bottom of the page, Sears printed a dare.

83
00:06:14,020 --> 00:06:19,080
If you think mail-order buying doesn't pay, give us one trial.

84
00:06:19,350 --> 00:06:24,050
And every autumn, the catalog grew a Christmas section.

85
00:06:24,070 --> 00:06:29,590
Toys. Dolls. Stockings stuffed with presents, ready to mail.

86
00:06:29,860 --> 00:06:35,270
In 1933, Sears printed its first separate Christmas catalog.

87
00:06:35,290 --> 00:06:38,430
Families had their own name for it,

88
00:06:38,450 --> 00:06:43,350
and in 1968 Sears made it official. The Wish Book.

89
00:06:43,620 --> 00:06:47,200
Maybe you remember it. The toys at the back.

90
00:06:47,220 --> 00:06:49,150
The corners folded down.

91
00:06:49,170 --> 00:06:53,880
The circles you drew around the things you hoped for.

92
00:06:54,150 --> 00:06:58,550
So, if you could buy the stove and the sewing machine by mail,

93
00:06:58,570 --> 00:07:01,740
could you buy the house to put them in?

94
00:07:07,410 --> 00:07:11,120
In 1908, Sears began selling houses.

95
00:07:11,140 --> 00:07:14,730
Not just the plans. The houses.

96
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:19,080
You picked one from a catalog called Modern Homes,

97
00:07:19,100 --> 00:07:24,340
and Sears sent it by rail. The lumber, cut to size and numbered.

98
00:07:24,360 --> 00:07:28,040
The nails, the doors, the windows, the paint.

99
00:07:28,060 --> 00:07:32,710
As many as 30,000 parts, and a book of instructions.

100
00:07:32,980 --> 00:07:36,670
Sears said a man of average abilities

101
00:07:36,690 --> 00:07:41,080
could put one together in about 90 days.

102
00:07:41,350 --> 00:07:46,010
Between 1908 and 1940, Sears sold somewhere between

103
00:07:46,030 --> 00:07:50,420
70,000 and 75,000 of them. No one knows exactly,

104
00:07:50,440 --> 00:07:54,870
because Sears didn't keep the sales records.

105
00:07:55,140 --> 00:07:59,200
Which brings us to a small town in Illinois.

106
00:07:59,470 --> 00:08:03,520
Carlinville sits about 40 miles south of Springfield,

107
00:08:03,540 --> 00:08:06,920
in the coal country of Macoupin County.

108
00:08:07,190 --> 00:08:12,360
In 1918, Standard Oil of Indiana opened coal mines near the town,

109
00:08:12,380 --> 00:08:15,550
and the miners needed somewhere to live.

110
00:08:15,570 --> 00:08:20,340
So the company ordered houses from Sears. Lots of them.

111
00:08:20,610 --> 00:08:24,320
192 houses, for about a million dollars.

112
00:08:24,340 --> 00:08:29,950
It's the largest order for Sears houses anyone knows of.

113
00:08:30,220 --> 00:08:32,710
Most of them went to Carlinville,

114
00:08:32,730 --> 00:08:36,360
onto a new neighborhood of about a dozen blocks.

115
00:08:36,380 --> 00:08:39,730
People called it the Standard Addition.

116
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:45,860
Here's one of the designs Sears was selling that year.

117
00:08:45,880 --> 00:08:51,960
The Carlin. Five rooms, a porch, and a balcony upstairs.

118
00:08:51,980 --> 00:08:53,120
$1,172.

119
00:08:53,390 --> 00:08:56,360
And Sears was proud of Carlinville.

120
00:08:56,380 --> 00:09:00,960
A few years later, it put the town in its own catalog.

121
00:09:00,980 --> 00:09:05,710
“We sell houses,” the page says. “In any quantity.”

122
00:09:05,980 --> 00:09:09,490
These are the street views it printed.

123
00:09:09,510 --> 00:09:13,600
The Standard Addition, new, around 1920.

124
00:09:13,870 --> 00:09:16,530
But the coal didn't last.

125
00:09:16,550 --> 00:09:22,920
In 1925, Standard Oil closed its mines near Carlinville.

126
00:09:23,190 --> 00:09:28,130
At the mining camp of Schoper nearby, local historians say

127
00:09:28,150 --> 00:09:32,750
most of the Sears houses were taken apart, loaded back

128
00:09:32,770 --> 00:09:36,260
onto boxcars, and left the way they came.

129
00:09:36,280 --> 00:09:39,220
Schoper is a ghost town today.

130
00:09:39,490 --> 00:09:42,530
But in Carlinville, the houses stayed.

131
00:09:42,550 --> 00:09:45,510
About 150 of them are still standing.

132
00:09:45,530 --> 00:09:49,690
It's thought to be the largest group of Sears houses

133
00:09:49,710 --> 00:09:52,340
in one place in the country.

134
00:09:52,610 --> 00:09:56,140
And there are Sears houses all over America

135
00:09:56,160 --> 00:09:58,280
whose owners have no idea.

136
00:09:58,300 --> 00:10:02,070
If your house was built between 1908 and 1940,

137
00:10:02,090 --> 00:10:04,870
look in the basement or the attic,

138
00:10:04,890 --> 00:10:07,980
for numbers stamped on the beams.

139
00:10:08,250 --> 00:10:11,870
There's a harder part of the story, too.

140
00:10:11,890 --> 00:10:15,970
From about 1912, Sears lent buyers the money.

141
00:10:15,990 --> 00:10:19,970
When the Depression came, many couldn't pay.

142
00:10:19,990 --> 00:10:25,250
Sears had to sell off about $11 million in loans gone bad,

143
00:10:25,270 --> 00:10:29,560
and at the end of 1933, it stopped lending.

144
00:10:29,830 --> 00:10:32,870
The house catalog ended in 1940.

145
00:10:32,890 --> 00:10:36,980
By then, Sears had found a new way to sell.

146
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:41,290
And this one didn't need the mail at all.

147
00:10:46,960 --> 00:10:50,720
By the 1920s, the farm family had a car.

148
00:10:50,740 --> 00:10:54,520
And the car could take them to town.

149
00:10:54,790 --> 00:10:59,100
A retired Army general named Robert Wood joined Sears,

150
00:10:59,120 --> 00:11:02,550
and he believed the future was stores.

151
00:11:02,820 --> 00:11:07,990
On 2 February 1925, the first Sears store opened,

152
00:11:08,010 --> 00:11:12,200
inside the catalog plant in Chicago.

153
00:11:12,470 --> 00:11:17,440
By the end of 1927, there were 27.

154
00:11:17,460 --> 00:11:21,210
By 1932, more than 300.

155
00:11:21,480 --> 00:11:24,500
And in 1931, for the first time,

156
00:11:24,520 --> 00:11:28,510
the stores sold more than the catalog.

157
00:11:28,780 --> 00:11:35,260
Here's the Sears building in Allentown, Pennsylvania, in 1929.

158
00:11:35,530 --> 00:11:40,660
In town after town, Sears became the store on Main Street.

159
00:11:40,680 --> 00:11:44,970
Later, the big store at the end of the mall.

160
00:11:45,240 --> 00:11:49,700
It sold its own names. Kenmore had first appeared

161
00:11:49,720 --> 00:11:52,260
on a sewing machine in 1913.

162
00:11:52,280 --> 00:11:55,730
In 1927, it went on a washing machine.

163
00:11:55,750 --> 00:12:00,970
The same year, Sears started selling Craftsman tools.

164
00:12:01,240 --> 00:12:04,600
Maybe that's the wrench in your garage.

165
00:12:04,870 --> 00:12:08,370
So how big could one company get?

166
00:12:14,040 --> 00:12:19,310
By the 1960s, Sears was the biggest retailer in America.

167
00:12:19,330 --> 00:12:25,100
And in 1970, it started building a headquarters to match.

168
00:12:25,370 --> 00:12:30,750
When it opened in 1973, the Sears Tower in Chicago

169
00:12:30,770 --> 00:12:36,150
was the tallest building in the world. 1,451 feet.

170
00:12:36,170 --> 00:12:41,300
It would hold the title for nearly 25 years.

171
00:12:41,570 --> 00:12:45,650
Sears moved its offices out of the old catalog plant

172
00:12:45,670 --> 00:12:49,040
on the West Side, and up into the sky.

173
00:12:49,310 --> 00:12:54,130
But down on the ground, shopping was changing again.

174
00:12:54,400 --> 00:12:59,510
A discount chain from Arkansas called Walmart was growing fast,

175
00:12:59,530 --> 00:13:02,280
in exactly the kind of small towns

176
00:13:02,300 --> 00:13:04,890
Sears had built its business on.

177
00:13:04,910 --> 00:13:09,530
And new stores that sold just one thing, like Home Depot,

178
00:13:09,550 --> 00:13:12,040
sold more of it, for less.

179
00:13:12,310 --> 00:13:17,420
Sears tried to be everything at once. It owned Allstate insurance.

180
00:13:17,440 --> 00:13:20,300
It bought a stockbroker, Dean Witter.

181
00:13:20,320 --> 00:13:25,440
It launched the Discover card. Its stores kept losing ground.

182
00:13:25,710 --> 00:13:30,280
By the start of the 1990s, Walmart had passed Sears

183
00:13:30,300 --> 00:13:33,820
as the biggest retailer in America.

184
00:13:34,090 --> 00:13:37,400
What would Sears give up to survive?

185
00:13:43,070 --> 00:13:46,800
In 1992, Sears lost $3.9 billion.

186
00:13:46,820 --> 00:13:52,350
It was the biggest loss a North American retailer

187
00:13:52,370 --> 00:13:54,790
had ever reported.

188
00:13:55,060 --> 00:13:59,220
The catalog was part of the problem. By one account,

189
00:13:59,240 --> 00:14:03,150
it was losing as much as a million dollars a day.

190
00:14:03,170 --> 00:14:07,160
Printing a book of some 1,500 pages and mailing it

191
00:14:07,180 --> 00:14:11,250
to millions of homes had become too expensive.

192
00:14:11,520 --> 00:14:16,930
On 25 January 1993, Sears announced the end.

193
00:14:16,950 --> 00:14:19,020
No more Big Book.

194
00:14:19,040 --> 00:14:25,460
113 stores would close, and 50,000 jobs would go.

195
00:14:25,730 --> 00:14:29,880
That year, Sears also began moving out of its tower,

196
00:14:29,900 --> 00:14:33,560
to new offices in the suburbs of Chicago.

197
00:14:33,830 --> 00:14:38,730
And on 5 July 1994, a man in Washington State

198
00:14:38,750 --> 00:14:44,200
started a company to sell books over the internet.

199
00:14:44,220 --> 00:14:47,110
It would become Amazon.

200
00:14:47,380 --> 00:14:51,280
It's easy now to say Sears threw the future away.

201
00:14:51,300 --> 00:14:54,630
But in 1993, almost nobody shopped online.

202
00:14:54,650 --> 00:14:58,950
The danger Sears could see was Walmart and Home Depot,

203
00:14:58,970 --> 00:15:00,310
not the internet.

204
00:15:00,330 --> 00:15:04,940
Ending the catalog was a decision to save the stores.

205
00:15:05,210 --> 00:15:08,830
Could the stores alone keep Sears alive?

206
00:15:14,500 --> 00:15:18,850
In 2004, Kmart announced it would buy Sears.

207
00:15:18,870 --> 00:15:24,420
Kmart was controlled by an investor named Eddie Lampert.

208
00:15:24,440 --> 00:15:27,400
The deal closed in March 2005.

209
00:15:27,420 --> 00:15:32,680
Together, Sears and Kmart had about 3,500 stores.

210
00:15:32,950 --> 00:15:37,160
Lampert cut costs and looked for value in the real estate

211
00:15:37,180 --> 00:15:40,500
under the stores. His critics said too little

212
00:15:40,520 --> 00:15:43,250
went back into the stores themselves.

213
00:15:43,270 --> 00:15:47,370
His supporters said the shoppers had already gone.

214
00:15:47,640 --> 00:15:51,280
One by one, the famous names were sold.

215
00:15:51,300 --> 00:15:55,870
Craftsman went to Stanley Black & Decker in 2017.

216
00:15:55,890 --> 00:15:59,560
DieHard batteries, two years later.

217
00:15:59,830 --> 00:16:06,700
On 15 October 2018, Sears Holdings filed for bankruptcy.

218
00:16:06,720 --> 00:16:11,910
It had 687 Sears and Kmart stores left.

219
00:16:12,180 --> 00:16:17,010
Lampert bought what remained at auction, through a new company.

220
00:16:17,030 --> 00:16:20,580
And the closing-sale signs kept going up.

221
00:16:20,850 --> 00:16:26,970
This is the Sears in Hialeah, Florida, in February 2020.

222
00:16:26,990 --> 00:16:29,020
Last five days.

223
00:16:29,290 --> 00:16:33,380
By the end of 2025, there were five Sears stores

224
00:16:33,400 --> 00:16:36,010
left in the United States.

225
00:16:36,280 --> 00:16:37,900
So what's left?

226
00:16:43,570 --> 00:16:46,150
Go back to Carlinville.

227
00:16:46,420 --> 00:16:48,730
The coal mines are long gone.

228
00:16:48,750 --> 00:16:52,280
The trains still stop at the little station,

229
00:16:52,300 --> 00:16:56,540
and the courthouse still stands over the square.

230
00:16:56,810 --> 00:17:01,140
And in the Standard Addition, about 150 Sears houses

231
00:17:01,160 --> 00:17:03,310
are still somebody's home.

232
00:17:03,330 --> 00:17:08,550
More than a hundred years after they came in on the train.

233
00:17:08,820 --> 00:17:11,670
Look around your own house again.

234
00:17:11,690 --> 00:17:14,530
The Craftsman wrench still turns.

235
00:17:14,550 --> 00:17:17,830
Kenmore is still a name on appliances.

236
00:17:17,850 --> 00:17:22,250
And in Chicago, the tower Sears built still stands,

237
00:17:22,270 --> 00:17:27,160
though since 2009 it's been called the Willis Tower.

238
00:17:27,430 --> 00:17:30,900
Sears had the future in its hands more than once.

239
00:17:30,920 --> 00:17:34,590
It brought the city store to the farm gate, by mail.

240
00:17:34,610 --> 00:17:39,680
It brought the store to the end of the road, when the car arrived.

241
00:17:39,950 --> 00:17:43,420
The third time, it let go.

242
00:17:43,690 --> 00:17:48,770
But a lot of it is still here. In the houses. In the toolboxes.

243
00:17:48,790 --> 00:17:52,820
And in the memory of a book that came in the mail,

244
00:17:52,840 --> 00:17:55,730
with the whole world inside it.

245
00:17:56,000 --> 00:18:01,560
Did your family order from the catalog, or live in a Sears house?

246
00:18:01,580 --> 00:18:06,850
Tell us in the comments. Next time: Rochester, and Kodak.
